<p>A brand style guide should include everything someone needs to make your business look and sound like itself without asking you: your logo versions and the rules for using them, your exact color codes, your fonts and what each one is for, your brand voice, the kind of photos you use, and real examples of all of it on things like business cards, email signatures and your website. If a sign shop, a printer or a new employee can open it and get it right the first time, it's doing its job.</p>

<p>Small businesses need one for a simple reason: your brand gets made by a lot of hands. The person who designs your truck wrap isn't the person who posts on Instagram, and neither of them is the person printing your menus. Without one shared reference, every version drifts a little. A style guide keeps your Albuquerque storefront, your Facebook page and your invoices all telling customers the same story.</p>

<h2>What is a brand style guide, really?</h2>

<p>Think of it as the instruction manual for your brand identity. Your logo, colors and fonts are the parts. The style guide explains how they fit together, what's allowed and what isn't. It's sometimes called brand guidelines, brand standards or a brand book. The name matters less than whether people actually use it.</p>

<p>Here's a real example. When DYAD refined the identity for Gorman Industries Inc., an Albuquerque company that has built trust since 1950, the project included a set of <a href="/immerse/gorman-rebrand-standards.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rebrand standards</a>. It explains why the brand was being realigned, shows each logo version, names the typeface and the weight to use for headings, subtitles and body text, lists the color palette by hex code and shows the brand applied to email signatures, invoices, letterhead, business cards and the website. That's the shape a useful guide takes: reasons, rules and examples.</p>

<h2>What should a brand style guide include?</h2>

<p>You don't need every section a global company has. You do need the ones that answer the questions people ask most often. Here's what to cover, in roughly the order people look for it.</p>

<h3>1. Your logo and its versions</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Every version you use:</strong> the full logo, a stacked version for square spaces, a horizontal or long version for banners, and a small mark or icon for social profiles and favicons.</li>
  <li><strong>Color versions:</strong> full color, one color, black, and white (reversed) for dark backgrounds.</li>
  <li><strong>Clear space and minimum size:</strong> how much breathing room the logo needs and how small it can go before it stops being readable.</li>
  <li><strong>What not to do:</strong> a few quick examples of stretching, recoloring, adding shadows or placing it on a busy photo.</li>
  <li><strong>Which file to use where:</strong> vector files (like AI, EPS, SVG or PDF) for signs, print and embroidery, and PNG or WebP files for screens.</li>
</ul>

<p>The Gorman standards, for example, show a full logo, submark, longform, stacked and watermark version, so whoever is designing a piece can pick the one that fits the space instead of squishing the main logo into it.</p>

<h3>2. Your color palette</h3>

<p>List each brand color with every code a vendor might ask for: HEX and RGB for screens, CMYK for print, and a Pantone match if you print on signs, shirts or packaging. Then say how the colors are used. Which one is your main color? Which one is just an accent? Which combinations should never sit next to each other?</p>

<p>It's also worth noting which text and background combinations are easy to read. The W3C's accessibility guidelines recommend a <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal body text</a>. Writing down the pairs that pass saves your web developer and social media person a lot of guesswork.</p>

<h3>3. Your fonts and what each one is for</h3>

<p>Name your fonts, where to get them and how to use them. Most small businesses need two, maybe three: one for headings, one for body text, and sometimes an accent font used sparingly. Spell out the roles, like "headings in bold all caps, body text in the regular weight." Also list a safe backup font for places where your real fonts won't load, like email or a shared document.</p>

<h3>4. Your brand voice and key messages</h3>

<p>This is the section most small business guides skip, and it's the one that matters most once other people start writing for you. Include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Three to five words that describe how you sound (for example: friendly, direct, local, knowledgeable).</li>
  <li>A short description of what you do and who you serve, written the way you'd say it to a customer.</li>
  <li>Words and phrases you always use, and ones you avoid.</li>
  <li>How you write your business name, your phone number format and your tagline.</li>
</ul>

<p>Vince sees this a lot: business owners treat strategy and messaging as a nice extra, when it's really the foundation that decides how everything else looks. He describes marketing strategy as "the entire skeletal structure beneath" the visuals. A style guide is a good place to write that foundation down, so your positioning, your core values and the promises you make to customers stay the same everywhere.</p>

<h3>5. Photo and graphic style</h3>

<p>Describe the look of your images. Real photos of your team, place and work, or illustrations? Bright and warm, or dark and moody? Close up product shots or wide shots of people? Add a few examples you love and a few you'd never post. If you use icons, patterns or textures, include them here too.</p>

<h3>6. Real examples in use</h3>

<p>Show the brand applied to the things you actually make: business cards, email signatures, invoices, letterhead, signs, social posts, a website page, a uniform or a vehicle. Examples answer questions that rules can't. When someone isn't sure how big the logo should be on a flyer, a finished example is the fastest answer.</p>

<h2>Why a small business needs a style guide</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Consistency builds recognition.</strong> When your sign, your website and your social posts all match, people connect them to the same business faster and remember you.</li>
  <li><strong>It saves time and money.</strong> Vendors stop guessing, you stop answering the same questions, and you pay for fewer reprints.</li>
  <li><strong>It protects the brand when you grow.</strong> New hires, new vendors and new locations all start from the same rules.</li>
  <li><strong>It makes outsourcing easier.</strong> Hand one file to a printer, a sign shop or a web developer instead of a folder of random logos.</li>
</ul>

<p>Consistency is also what makes slow, steady marketing work. It's a big part of <a href="/blog/grow-instagram-local-business">how a local business grows on Instagram without paid ads</a>: people need to recognize your posts before they start to trust them.</p>

<h2>How long should a style guide be?</h2>

<p>For most small businesses, a focused PDF is plenty: a page or two per topic, lots of visuals, very little filler. The Gorman Industries standards mentioned above run 12 pages, including the examples. A larger company with many locations or products may need more, but the goal is the same. If people have to dig to find your color codes, the guide is too long or badly organized.</p>

<h2>How to make one if your brand already exists</h2>

<p>You don't need a brand new logo to start a style guide. You can build one from what you already use:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Gather every version of your logo you can find and pick the best, highest quality file for each. If you only have a blurry JPG, that's a sign the logo itself may need to be rebuilt as a vector.</li>
  <li>Find your exact color codes from your printer, sign shop or website, and write them all down in one place.</li>
  <li>Identify your fonts. If you aren't sure what they are, ask whoever designed your logo or website.</li>
  <li>Write down the rules you already follow without thinking about them.</li>
  <li>Collect your best examples: the business card, post or page that looks most like "you."</li>
  <li>Share it with everyone who makes anything for your business, and update it when things change.</li>
</ol>

<p>If that process turns up a lot of inconsistency, it may be time to decide whether your brand needs a light refresh or a bigger change. Our post on <a href="/blog/rebrand-or-refresh-logo-albuquerque">rebrand vs. refresh</a> walks through how to tell the difference.</p>

